import{_ as t,o as s,c as n,a0 as o}from"./chunks/framework.ChlVJx0I.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"The resource list reports settings drift","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0047-the-resource-list-reports-settings-drift.md","filePath":"adr/0047-the-resource-list-reports-settings-drift.md","lastUpdated":1792414654000}'),r={name:"adr/0047-the-resource-list-reports-settings-drift.md"};function a(i,e,d,l,h,c){return s(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="the-resource-list-reports-settings-drift" tabindex="-1">The resource list reports settings drift <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-resource-list-reports-settings-drift" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The resource list reports settings drift&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Settings express intent: the resource arrays, the package filters, the extension-named keys. When a line does nothing, the intent dies silently: the user&#39;s model-router sat disabled by the package filter with a full fallback list and wait budget under its settings key, and the drift was found by hand in a retrospective over 12 weeks of sessions. The sibling drop of pruning, compress, and quota (ADR 0046) left the user&#39;s settings with package-filter entries naming files pi no longer loads. The unmet need is not toggling - the state commands show zero recorded use in the same corpus - it is knowing when the settings lie.</p><p>resource-toggle already owns the Toggle state of every resource: it resolves the whole resource catalog and reads both raw settings documents on every command. We decided: the drift check is part of the extension&#39;s job. A new pure module inside resource-toggle computes the findings from the raw settings documents and the resolved catalog, and the findings render as a read-only Drift group at the top of the interactive list, as a section of the headless tables and the <code>resource_toggle</code> tool output, and as one session-start notice when the findings are non-empty. Clean settings produce no group and no notice.</p><p>v1 reports exactly three classes:</p><ul><li><strong>Stale entry</strong>: an override pattern in a settings resource array, or any entry of a package filter, whose target matches no loaded resource and, where it resolves to a file path, names no file on disk. The matching uses the state machine&#39;s own pattern rules, resolved against the resource&#39;s own base (the resource&#39;s base dir, falling back to the scope base when the resource has none, for a resource-array entry, the package root for a filter entry), so a line pi ignores is what gets named.</li><li><strong>Conflicting entries</strong>: two entries in one array with the same target and opposite polarity, one force-including the resource the other force-excludes.</li><li><strong>Configured while disabled</strong>: an extension disabled in the effective view while a top-level settings key equal to its name in camelCase (<code>model-router</code> to <code>modelRouter</code>, single-word names to themselves) exists in a settings document. The key match is a name guess, not proof, so the finding is flagged possible: first-party extensions that break the convention degrade to this soft finding, and a settings key that merely shares a disabled extension&#39;s name does not read as a hard error.</li></ul><h2 id="considered-options" tabindex="-1">Considered Options <a class="header-anchor" href="#considered-options" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Considered Options&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A check inside resource-toggle (chosen).</strong> The extension already does the resolution and reads both raw documents on every command, so the check rides on existing seams: no new settings read, no new pi API, one pure module testable without a live session. The findings share the exact rendering surfaces the user already opens, and the state commands, the picker, the Toggle state machine, and the settings write path are untouched.</li><li><strong>A separate extension.</strong> A new extension would redo the resolution or reach into resource-toggle&#39;s internals, and a second surface reporting the same settings would drift from the list. The drift question has one answer: what the resource catalog says about the settings files.</li><li><strong>Pi core diagnostics.</strong> Pi validates the settings format, not resource semantics: whether a pattern matches a loaded resource, and which top-level keys belong to which extension, are questions only the resolver plus the catalog can answer.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consequences&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The resolution carries the raw settings documents (not just the resource arrays) so the soft class can read the extension-named keys without a new settings read.</li><li>An untrusted project covers the global scope only, matching what the rest of the resource view can read: the project file is unreadable, and its entries and keys are not findings.</li><li>The camelCase heuristic is exact on the mapped name. Until a v2 settings-key registry lets extensions declare the keys they read, a finding of this class stays possible: a registry would turn possible findings into definitive ones and surface the orphaned keys of deleted extensions, which v1 does not name.</li><li>Findings are deduplicated and sorted by class, scope, then text, so the notice and the tables are stable across runs and modes. Each finding names its scope - global, project, or the package source - so the fix lands in the right file.</li><li>The session-start notice fires once per pi process and points at /resources. The check names the drift; deleting the line is the user&#39;s edit. An in-session fix action is a v2 question.</li><li>Pi core settings unrelated to resources (compaction, theme, model blocks) are not checked: only resource arrays, package filters, and extension-named keys are in scope.</li></ul>',9)])])}const p=t(r,[["render",a]]);export{g as __pageData,p as default};
